    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d721">

                <pb facs="0980" n="968" sameAs="frus:frus1945v05/pg968"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.24/1–2745: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Kennan</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-01-27T17:00:00+03:00">January 27, 1945—5
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 8:30 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>250. In the matter of transfer of Lend-Lease supplies to third parties, discussed
                    in Department’s 115, January 18, 10 p.m., Ambassador Harriman, prior to his
                    departure, still had certain points on which he wished further clarification
                    before taking the matter up with the Soviet Government.</p>
                <p>In the belief that he may wish to raise these questions himself on his return, I
                    am taking no action at this time on the Department’s instruction under
                    reference.</p>
                <p>Meanwhile I wish to invite the Department’s attention not only to the proposed
                    sale of grain to Finland but also to the decision, announced in today’s Soviet
                    press, to give 60,000 tons of grain to the city of Warsaw (see my next following
                        telegram<anchor n="3" xml:id="d721-f1"/> in clear). This decision is being
                    given major propaganda build up in the Soviet press and no doubt in Poland as
                    well. I am sure that Ambassador Harriman would welcome an indication of the
                    attitude the Department would wish him to adopt on these specific
                    transactions.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kennan</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
